## The Process of Filing a Claim Under Lemon Law in CA.

- Call the manufacturer and report your defect. They will ask you to take your vehicle to an authorized repair facility.

- Document all your repairs, including:

- Issues with the vehicle

- Date of repairs

- Time spent in the repair facility

- Invoices

- Receipts

- Hire a competent Costa Mesa lemon law attorney.

## Lemon Law Coverage for Pre-Owned Vehicles

When sold with a warranty, California lemon law also extends to used vehicles.

## How Warranties on Used Vehicles from Dealerships Work

Generally, used car dealerships in California sell vehicles with a dealer warranty. It is usually valid for 30 days after the purchase date or before 1,000 miles accrue on the odometer.

You can file a claim under CA lemon law if your vehicle fits these criteria.

## What Are Your Rights as a Consumer Under the Lemon Law

Used car dealerships are required to repair any defects covered under the Lemon law in CA. If they fail to fix the issues, you may be able to file a claim for a buyback.

You can seek compensation for:

- The cost of the vehicle

- Official expenses _associated_ with the vehicle

- Incidental costs

- All legal and attorney fees

## What Does Buying a Vehicle “As Is” Mean

When you buy a vehicle “as is,” you agree to take on any problems the vehicle might encounter after you’ve purchased it. This potentially affects your rights to recover compensation under the Lemon law in CA.

However, for an “as is” sale to hold up legally, the dealership specifically needs to state that the vehicle is being sold “as is” on the buyer’s guide when showing the vehicle. If they fail to do so, you might still be able to file a claim.

